  "notes": "A 1960s-tracked debut and then a contemporary production turn, and the entry says \"both variants required\". This model holds them in one kit rather than in two modes: the snare is layered (the debut's high dry stick plus the later records' soft clap) with a rimshot option and a small displacement, and the hat base is weighted between the debut's no-carpet 8ths and the later 16ths. The band is the catalogue's own 67–120 and the keys are the named sharp-and-flat majors — F♯, G, A♭ and B — an unusual set the research calls out specifically. Harmony is the debut's \"50s/60s doo-wop-diatonic (`I–vi–IV–V`, 12-bar blues, `I–IV` two-chord vamps with dominant 7ths)\", both families written out by the entry, with the triad weight high; the later m9/maj9 extensions are what keeps the seventh weight from collapsing. Melody is \"smooth mid-tenor, low note-density, long phrases, minimal melisma, with a period-appropriate straight-tone delivery\", which is the legato articulation, density 2–5, a high `chordToneBias` and a near-zero octave jump. The \"female group-vocal answers\" are the counter at +12. Bass is \"upright or flatwound electric bass on the debut, walking in quarters with a slap-back feel; played electric with light sub-doubling on the later material\" — walking, so an independent riff with chromatic passing tones rather than a kick mirror. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number and all melody figures; the research marks the debut's drum-tracking detail as inference. ⚠ The light sub-doubling on the later records is not authored as a `bass808` block — it doubles a played bass.",
